Date: Sat, 20 Mar 93 00:38:22 GMT
From: "Stephen J. Whitrow" <sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk>
Subject: reason

>> The schoolmen of the Middle Ages may have claimed that their beliefs were
>> based on reason, but they were hampered by a scarcity of empirical evidence.

>   Their beliefs were based on reason, that was the whole problem; they thought
> that reason was superior to empirical evidence in describing the physical 
> world. Galileo's point was that it doesn't matter how satisfactory the reasons
> are that heavier objects should fall faster than lighter ones, if it doesn't
> happen that way then it doesn't happen that way. Science was therefore a 
> revolt against the rationalistic excesses of medaevil scholasticism. Put that
> in your pipe and smoke it.

> From: shunwton@reading.ac.uk

My point here was that as experience and empirical evidence built up, there
was less room for these "rationalistic excesses". Nowadays, anyone who 
claimed to know how many angels could dance on a pinhead would quite rightly
be regarded as a complete plank. 

Arch-empiricism is a rather unrewarding, inefficient epistemology. It greatly
reduces the diversity of memes in the meme pool. And if all knowledge has to
be derived from experience, then every time you want to add two numbers (say
7838 and 8308) that you can't remember adding before, you have to go and get
around 17,000 buttons and do it manually. 

Also, denying reason is not very utilitarian in the medical sense; it is used
as an excuse for bureaucratic excesses. When one drug results in unfortunate
side-effects regulators will jump on this as an excuse to make it so 
expensive to launch a new drug that hundreds of thousands or millions have
probably died and suffered unnecessary pain as a result. Of course, the 
regulators are not interested in the greatest good for the greatest number.
Their concern is with their own power and careers. 

One 'meta'-point here; if we quote from two distinct posters in the same post
we would normally show their names, or indent, to avoid giving the impression
that both quotes came from the same poster.  

