Date: Fri, 19 Mar 1993 18:48:00 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Law of the Dominant Paradigm

 
hammar@cs.unm.edu:
>    Facts which contradict societys strongest meme
>    sets die out spontaniously (in terms of general publication). 

This observation leads to my Law of the Dominant Paradigm:

The chance that a proposition is true increases with increasing evidence,
and decreases with increasing popularity.

I came up with this theory before I came across Tim May's k-space 
mining metaphor, but his model illustrates the law well.  Gold 
will lie in the ground until evidence for it is found, at which
time there will be a gold rush, and prospectors will abound, finding 
even more evidence for gold in that area, until it is depleted.  
Similarly, the more popular an idea, the more people are arguing for 
it and finding evidence for it.  As a result, where there are two 
propositions with the same amount of evidence, the one that has been 
less popular has probably had less of its evidence unearthed, thus 
has more total evidence, and is therefore more likely to be true.  

As an extropian I want to expand my mind into the new, fertile
gold mines of k-space -- to badly mix metaphors :-).  Thus
I seek out that which has been less popular, but nevertheless has 
accumulated good evidence.  A corollary to this Law is that it
applies not just to truth, but also to value.  One the most basic 
investment strategies follows the law -- buy that which has good 
value but nevertheless is less popular at the moment.  "Contrarians" 
concentrate on the popularity clause of this law, and often do well.

Nick Szabo					szabo@techbook.com

