Date: Fri, 19 Mar 93 20:40:14 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: IPS: Invitational Polite Society

I detect a little annoyance at me in Robin Hanson's last posting on The
Polite Society. Perhaps I need to expand on my views, and offer my own
version. Some points:

* the idea of new structures and new rules for mailing lists is a timely one.

* for a variety of reasons--mostly having to do with enforceability--most
of Robin's ideas won't work well with non-mailing lists. For example,
losing "reading rights to newsgroups" is essentially unenforceable. (Note
that "losing posting rights" *is* enforceable by having anyone who spots a
forbidden post call this to the group's attention for more severe action.
Reputations matter. Not that I favor this tack, though.)

* the fundamental problem is that getting shot hurts differently for
different people. Lurkers won't much care, while others may go into posting
withdrawal shock. The rules of the Polite Society fail to handle this
disparity. (I've tried to imagine pricing schemes, such as buying
"insurance" against being shot (those at high risk end up paying more, for
example), or negotiating some kind of Clarke tax scheme, but it just gets
very complicated. And the list software can't handle monetary transactions,
anyway.)

* several of us have described scenarios in which lurkers or assholes can
knock folks off the list with little cost to themselves. THIS IS A FATAL
FLAW, imho.

* new "epicycles" which limit the number of shots, the number of repeat
shots, etc., slow down the descent into chaos, but don't stop it.

What, if anything, can be done?

The key is to make membership in the Society a desirable thing, something
that is not to be lightly thrown away. (In real shootouts, the price paid
is the ultimate one...and yet people still do it.)

Some ideas:

- membership should be *by invitation only*. This keeps out the assholes
and the fools and makes being kicked out of the Society a negative outcome.

- the invitational aspect captures the "politeness" aspect more accurately,
I think, than the "shooting" aspect does. (It echoes how people are invited
to join prestigious societies, academic clubs, debating clubs, etc.). (BTW,
even if my idea here is "shot down," so to speak, I'd like to see us
consider making the Extropians list an "by invitation only" list, with some
mechanism for disinviting the mostly-disruptive posters. I believe only one
person has been completely kicked off the list in its history. This seems
too low.)

- when some critical mass of members is achieved, the IPS can mostly
respond publicly to the postings of its members. Of course, any member
should be able to pick and choose whom he responds to--except in the case
described below.

- offenders can be dropped without recourse to "letter of the law" rules
(exploited by "net.lawyers" like I was playing in my posts about following
the letter of the law in Robin's game to wreck his system). A vote by the
existing members can be used to kick someone out of the group.

- a finer-grained response to rude offenders (compared to kicking them out)
is to temporarily stop responding to their posts--this require no change to
the List software and is itself enforced on the honor principle. Call this
"shunning." (Presumably a kicked-out member would be shunned permanently,
or at least for some much-longer time. Maybe for 3 months or so. Enough to
make it painful.)

Here's a scenario:

Alice, Bob, Charles, Deke, Edy, Fred, and Gail form the nucleus of The
Invitational Polite Society (IPS). They agree to avoid flames, baiting
posts, debates of the basics, irrational diatribes about voodoo and ghosts,
and so on. The exact rules are *not important*. In fact, what they agree on
will change over time.

Alice writes a rude post, perhaps to Bob. Bob asks for a vote. Four of the
seven members vote to "censure" Alice. This could be, for example, a
decision to *not publicly respond to* any of Alice's posts for, say, a
week. (Alice is free to post, all are free to read, and hence no special
list software is needed, no centralized management is needed, and the only
act of judging needed was the original vote.)

Imagine that Charles, who perhaps voted with Alice in the vote, decides to
publicly respond to a post by Alice. This violates the agreeement the IPS
was based on. So Deke, for example, files a charge against Charles and a
vote is held. Perhaps Charles is himself "shunned" for some time, or
perhaps he is kicked out of the IPS for violating its rules.

(Some of this sounds a lot like our own list, right? Well, in many ways
it's like our list, except that we don't have votes on innocence or guilt.
Think of this as a way to allow "sublists" to be created without any new
software.)

Members of the IPS can act sort of like a "collective kill file." They may
even exchange lists of other persons who may be invited to join their
group, or who've written posts which are especially good, etc. (This is all
informal.)

But like Robin's proposal, this proposal could get out of hand and become
burdened with new rules and complicated lists of those who are being
shunned, those who are being considered for membership, etc. The classic
computer science question arises: how does it scale?

Keeping it simple has some advantages. Just a bunch of people who read
everything they wish to and only "shun" their own members who have been
deemed rude or foolish or whatever. I think this is what Robin was aiming
for.

This is the idea. No real rules...the rules are voted on by the members. 

Comments?

-Tim May, IPS member since 3-19-93.
